WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:10.040
Now, before we dive into how the indexes works in databases, let's understand first what happens to

00:10.080 --> 00:11.280
the database tables.

00:11.280 --> 00:17.360
If you don't use any index when you create a new table in your database, like for example, the customers

00:17.360 --> 00:21.320
table where you have, let's say 20 customers inside this table.

00:21.320 --> 00:27.160
What you're going to see at the client side is like spreadsheets, like a table with rows and columns.

00:27.160 --> 00:30.720
But behind the scenes, the database store it a bit differently.

00:30.720 --> 00:37.600
It can store the data in a data file on the disk, and inside this file, the data can be stored inside

00:37.600 --> 00:39.600
blocks called pages.

00:39.600 --> 00:43.960
So it's not like rows and columns that are stored inside data files.

00:43.960 --> 00:46.720
And inside the data files we have pages.

00:46.720 --> 00:48.120
So what is a page?

00:48.360 --> 00:52.320
A page is the unit of data storage in a database.

00:52.320 --> 00:55.640
And it is a fixed size of eight kilobytes.

00:55.680 --> 00:59.280
Where the SQL database can store anything inside it.

00:59.320 --> 01:06.170
It can store inside it the rows of your tables or columns, metadata indexes, and every time you are

01:06.170 --> 01:11.650
interacting with your data, the SQL is reading and writing to those pages.

01:11.650 --> 01:16.930
So as you can see, the SQL is not storing the data inside like rows and columns.

01:16.930 --> 01:21.850
So if you are running a query, the SQL is not like selecting a specific column.

01:22.050 --> 01:24.090
It always fits a database.

01:24.090 --> 01:27.490
In order to read the rows inside this page.

01:27.490 --> 01:32.010
And the main two tabs that we're going to learn is the data page and the index page.

01:32.010 --> 01:35.890
So how the database looks like it is divided into multiple sections.

01:35.890 --> 01:41.330
The first section is the page header, where the database can store key information about the metadata,

01:41.370 --> 01:44.090
like the page ID, and it has the following formats.

01:44.130 --> 01:49.370
It starts with the file ID like one, and then we have a unique number for each page.

01:49.370 --> 01:51.170
So for example 150.

01:51.210 --> 01:54.650
So the page header is a fixed size of 96 bytes.

01:54.690 --> 01:57.570
Now to the next section we can have a variable size.

01:57.770 --> 02:00.450
This is where your data row is going to be stored.

02:00.450 --> 02:03.980
So your actual data are raw is going to be stored in this section.

02:03.980 --> 02:09.180
And the SQL is going to try and fit as many rows as it can in one single page.

02:09.180 --> 02:12.220
And this of course depends on the size of each row.

02:12.220 --> 02:18.780
So if you have like a large table where the rows are really big so SQL can fit only few rows in one

02:18.780 --> 02:19.580
single page.

02:19.620 --> 02:24.140
And now moving on to the last section in the data page we have the offset array.

02:24.300 --> 02:28.740
This is like a quick index for the rows stored inside this page.

02:28.740 --> 02:36.060
It keeps track of where each rows begins, so that the SQL can easily locate a specific row without

02:36.060 --> 02:39.700
having SQL, like scanning the entire page in order to find a row.

02:39.700 --> 02:42.300
So this is the structure of the data page.

02:42.460 --> 02:47.420
And this is exactly how the SQL stores data inside the databases.

02:47.420 --> 02:51.220
So now back to our example where we have the customers table and 20 rows.

02:51.220 --> 02:54.020
So let's see how SQL is going to be creating those pages.

02:54.060 --> 02:57.100
Now if you are not using any index in this table.

02:57.100 --> 03:03.480
So now what can happen is SQL can insert the data inside those pages as you are inserting the data inside

03:03.480 --> 03:04.360
the customers.

03:04.360 --> 03:10.120
So maybe first you are inserting the customers like 12, five, 15, six, seven, and it's going to

03:10.160 --> 03:12.880
insert it to the data pages exactly like that.

03:12.880 --> 03:17.480
So that means SQL is just inserting the data as you insert it to the table.

03:17.480 --> 03:21.080
So let's say each data page is like fitting only five rows.

03:21.080 --> 03:27.240
So after we insert five customers it's going to go and create another data page for the next rows.

03:27.240 --> 03:31.480
So in the next page the SQL is going to insert the next five customers.

03:31.480 --> 03:36.560
And once it's full it's going to create another data page in order to start adding the next customer

03:36.560 --> 03:41.200
until we have like for example, four pages for that 20 customers.

03:41.200 --> 03:46.560
So now if you check the customers inside those four pages, you see that they are not sorted at all.

03:46.600 --> 03:50.600
And that's because in this scenario we are not using any index.

03:50.600 --> 03:53.840
So we call this structure as a heap structure.

03:53.840 --> 03:57.960
So a heap table is a table without a clustered index.

03:57.960 --> 04:02.770
That means the rows are stored randomly without any particular order.

04:02.810 --> 04:08.890
This is not really bad because it's going to be very quick to insert data inside this table.

04:08.930 --> 04:12.410
But of course, finding something from this table is going to be very slow.

04:12.410 --> 04:14.210
So this is the first trade off.

04:14.250 --> 04:18.450
You have a very fast rides but a very bad reads.

04:18.490 --> 04:23.730
Think about it like you are throwing all your papers in a drawer without organizing them.

04:23.730 --> 04:26.530
So you can toss things very quickly in this drawer.

04:26.570 --> 04:31.930
But if you want to search for a specific paper later, it can be a very long process until you find

04:31.930 --> 04:34.090
it because nothing's in order.

04:34.210 --> 04:36.370
So now let's see how the SQL can handle.

04:36.370 --> 04:38.290
If you read something from this table.

04:38.330 --> 04:41.770
Let's say that you are searching for the customer with the ID 14.

04:41.770 --> 04:45.570
So now SQL has totally no idea where to find this customer.

04:45.570 --> 04:50.290
So SQL can start fishing each database and start scanning each row.

04:50.290 --> 04:53.290
So it's going to start with the first data page and start scanning.

04:53.410 --> 04:55.810
Well SQL will not find 14 here.

04:55.810 --> 05:02.020
So it's going to go to the next page and start scanning as well searching for the id14 and nothing can

05:02.020 --> 05:02.420
be found.

05:02.420 --> 05:04.580
The same thing for the third page as well.

05:04.620 --> 05:11.100
SQL will not find 14, so SQL going to go to the last data page and there after scanning four rows in

05:11.100 --> 05:11.820
this data page.

05:11.860 --> 05:14.860
Finally SQL going to find the customer number 14.

05:14.900 --> 05:16.900
And it's going to return it for the clients.

05:16.980 --> 05:24.100
So as you can see in order to find one customer, SQL did read four different pages and scanned like

05:24.100 --> 05:25.420
19 rows.

05:25.460 --> 05:30.620
In order to find the customer and this process we call it full table scan.

05:30.660 --> 05:37.820
So the full table scans means SQL is scanning the entire table, page by page and row by row in order

05:37.820 --> 05:39.260
to find specific row.

05:39.300 --> 05:44.060
And of course, for this table, maybe it's not a big deal, but if you have like a big table where

05:44.060 --> 05:49.300
you have like hundreds of thousands or maybe millions of rows, searching through the heap structure

05:49.340 --> 05:52.740
can be very painful and slow in order to locate one row.

05:52.780 --> 05:56.740
And here exactly why we need indexes in SQL databases.

05:56.860 --> 06:00.980
So let's understand the first type of indexes the clustered index.
